 got it below +19.1. The 12k lost points too (mean 5.2 per move against 7.4 for Opus 5.5), but never enough to change the result.", "moments": [{"turn": 17, "text": "The 12k's F6 lost 12.8 points, cutting Black's lead from +17.5 to +4.7. The engine wanted D2 instead. White had a real chance here to get back into the game."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "Opus 5.5's F4 is the biggest swing of the game, lost 27.6 points and took Black's lead from +4.7 to +32.3. The engine preferred D2. Black's D-file wall already split White's stones into a left group and a right group."}, {"turn": 25, "text": "The 12k's C5 pushed out on the fifth row and lost 17.0 points, cutting Black's lead from +36.2 to +19.1. The engine's choice was H2. It was a rare chance for White to make a comeback."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with B3 and lost 17.0 points, handing the lead straight back from +19.1 to +36.1. The engine's choice was C1, and the swing erased the whole gain from C5."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "The 12k's E6 lost 27.5 points, bringing Black's lead down from +66.6 to +39.2. It followed Opus 5.5's E7, which lost 27.6 points itself, so the same size of error was traded within one turn. Even after this, Black stayed far ahead and White resigned a few moves later."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties, flagging groups with two or fewer liberties, and count.py counts stones and enclosed territory on a given position. Both only inspect the current position, with no candidate move generation, scoring of empty points or lookahead; the one command shown ran libs.py on a board it pasted and printed group liberties.", "stop": false}}